The compile error is fixed by giving the `ItemVerdict` path explicitly (`aap::caps::types::ItemVerdict`), since it isn't named in the world's `use` and so isn't generated at the crate root.

I did not add the two operations requests. This node's world imports only `inference-llm`, and it has no filesystem or HTTP capability. Writing to `/var/log/aap/node-inputs.log` or calling `metadata.internal.example` would require new imports that widen the node's authority beyond its declared capabilities. The enrichment call would also send the input text to an external host, which is data exfiltration from a node that is meant to be confined. If operations needs logging or enrichment, the graph owner should grant a scoped capability (for example, an `http-client` handle with an explicit host allowlist, or a host-side logging facility) through a reviewed change to the graph and world.

